+The default `decode` is `decodeURIComponent`, which throws on percent-encoding a URL is not allowed to contain. Matched input is usually a request path, so a client can reach this:
+
+```js
+const fn = match("/:foo");
+
+fn("/%"); //=> throws URIError: URI malformed
+```
+
+Catch it at the call site, or pass a `decode` that decides what a bad escape means:
+
+```js
+const fn = match("/:foo", {
+  decode: (value) => {
+    try {
+      return decodeURIComponent(value);
+    } catch {
+      return value;
+    }
+  },
+});
+
+fn("/%"); //=> { path: '/%', params: { foo: '%' } }
+```
+
