 An effort has been made to ensure ambiguous paths from previous releases throw an error. This means you might be seeing an error when things worked before.
 
+A failure caused by the path itself is thrown as a `PathError`: every parse error, and the structural errors `pathToRegexp` reports, `Missing text before ... param` and `Too many path combinations`. It extends `TypeError`, so a `catch` that already tests for a `TypeError` keeps working, and it carries the offending path on `originalPath`:
+
+```js
+const { parse, PathError } = require("path-to-regexp");
+
+let caught;
+try {
+  parse("/:");
+} catch (error) {
+  caught = error;
+}
+
+caught instanceof PathError; //=> true
+caught.originalPath; //=> "/:"
+caught.message; //=> "Missing parameter name at index 2: /:; visit https://git.new/pathToRegexpError for info"
+```
+
+`originalPath` is `undefined` when the failing path was a `TokenData` object built by hand, because there is no source string to point at. Failures about anything other than the path stay plain `TypeError`s: an option value (`Expected "delimiter" to be a non-empty string`), a token object carrying an unknown `type`, and the params handed to a compile function (`Missing parameters: a`, `Expected "foo" to be a non-empty array`). A bad percent-escape in matched input surfaces as whatever `decode` throws, `URIError` from the default.
+
