+/**
+ * `recheck` ends each search stage on whichever comes first, a step budget or
+ * a wall clock. The clocks are the problem, in both directions: a busy machine
+ * runs one out while the matching itself is fine and the case fails, and a
+ * search cut short reports `safe`, which is what 146 of the 151 assertions
+ * below expect - so a starved run can turn most of the ReDoS stage green
+ * without checking anything. Of the 5 that expect `vulnerable`, 4 go through
+ * the fuzzer and only the exponential sentinel is decided by the automaton
+ * checker, which never looks at a clock. Both were seen: with every core loaded this file once
+ * failed 15 of its 101 cases, and once returned `safe` for the polynomial
+ * source of `/:a--:b--:c`, against an assertion of `vulnerable`, on an
+ * unmodified tree.
+ *
+ * So the search stages are bounded by their step limits alone. Their clocks
+ * are disabled rather than widened, because widening only moves the machine
+ * speed at which the verdict changes. `randomSeed` is left alone: it already
+ * defaults to 0, so the genetic search is seeded deterministically and the
+ * clocks were the only source of run-to-run drift.
+ *
+ * Two clocks stay. `timeout` bounds the whole check and reports `timeout`,
+ * which fails an assertion loudly instead of passing as `safe`, so it is a
+ * backstop rather than a silent degradation. `attackTimeout` is the oracle's
+ * own threshold, not a budget: raising it to 10s reports that same polynomial
+ * source as safe, which is the gate quietly going blind.
+ */
+const STEP_BOUNDED = {
+  timeout: 120_000,
+  seedingTimeout: null,
+  incubationTimeout: null,
+};
+
